<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henry Purcell</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1659–<a href="/years/1695">1695</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henry Purcell was an English composer and organist of the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> era whose music, English in style, incorporated Italian and French elements. A former chorister of the Chapel Royal, he became organist of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a> in 1679, when John Blow resigned the office in his favour, and organist of the Chapel Royal in 1682. His works include more than 100 songs, the opera <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dido">Dido</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneas">Aeneas</a>, the semi-operas Dioclesian, King Arthur and The Fairy-Queen, odes such as Come Ye Sons of Art, and music for the funeral of Queen Mary in 1695. He died at his home in Marsham Street in November 1695, at the height of his career, and is buried beside the organ in Westminster Abbey.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-purcell.jpg" alt="Henry Purcell" width="330" height="395" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Closterman · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Henry_Purcell_(cropped).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The music writer Percy Scholes found likenesses to an early plainsong melody, to galliard dance tunes, to a keyboard piece by John Bull (1619) and to pieces by <b>Henry Purcell</b>, and recommended crediting the tune as &quot;traditional&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/god-save-the-king">God Save the King</a></li><li><blockquote>The melody of &quot;Lillibullero&quot; is attributed to <b>Henry Purcell</b>, though it is unclear whether he composed it or arranged it; it was first published in The Delightful Companion, John Playford's method book for the recorder, in 1686, and no original manuscript survives.</blockquote><a href="/songs/lillibullero">Lillibullero</a></li><li><blockquote>A Voluntary on the Old Hundredth for organ is ascribed to John Blow or to his pupil <b>Henry Purcell</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/old-100th">Old 100th</a></li></ul></section>
